        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-unflavored" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Go Unflavored</a>
          <a href="#taste-reality" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Whey vs Plant Taste</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#isolate-concentrate-casein" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Isolate, Concentrate or Casein</a>
          <a href="#heat-baking" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Heat and Baking</a>
          <a href="#coffee-oats-smoothies" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Coffee, Oats and Smoothies</a>
          <a href="#reading-the-label" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Reading the Label</a>
          <a href="#flavor-it-yourself" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Flavor It Yourself</a>
          <a href="#cost-dose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Cost and Dose</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An unflavored protein powder is the most useful tub you can own and the least exciting one to drink on its own. With no sweetener, no flavoring and often a single ingredient, it disappears into coffee, oats, smoothies, yogurt, soups and baking, and it lets you control exactly what goes into your food. The catch is that plain protein tastes of itself: faintly milky for whey, noticeably earthy for pea. This guide ranks the best unflavored protein powder options on what the label can prove, explains what buyers report they actually taste like, and covers what heat does to protein when you cook with it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-unflavored" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Go Unflavored
          </h2>

          <p>
            The main reason to buy unflavored protein is versatility. A vanilla or chocolate powder decides what your food tastes like; an unflavored one does not. You can stir it into savory dishes such as soup, mashed potatoes or a bowl of rice and beans, and into sweet ones such as oatmeal, yogurt, pancake batter and smoothies, without fighting a flavor that does not belong. One tub can replace two or three flavored ones for anyone who cooks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second reason is sweeteners. Almost every flavored powder contains sucralose, acesulfame potassium, stevia, monk fruit or sugar alcohols, and plenty of people either dislike the taste or would rather avoid them. Stevia's bitter finish is the most common complaint in flavored powder reviews, and sugar alcohols such as erythritol can upset some stomachs in larger amounts. Unflavored powders skip all of that. You sweeten to taste, with sugar, honey, fruit or nothing at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third reason is a short ingredient list. The best flavorless protein powder options contain one ingredient, or one plus a small amount of sunflower or soy lecithin to help it mix. No gums, no natural flavors, no colors, no added enzymes or blends. That makes them easy to check for allergens and suits people on elimination diets, people with sensitive digestion, and anyone who simply wants to know exactly what they are eating. Our guide to the <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> goes further into short-label and contaminant-tested picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unflavored is also often cheaper per 25 g of protein, because there is no flavoring system to add and because some brands price it as their base product. It is not the right choice for everyone. If you mainly drink protein shakes with water and want them to taste good with no extra effort, a flavored powder is the better buy, and our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide ranks the ones buyers like most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste-reality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Unflavored Whey Protein vs Plant: The Taste Reality
          </h2>

          <p>
            Unflavored does not mean tasteless. Buyers consistently describe unflavored whey protein as mild and faintly milky, a little like powdered milk with a slightly sour or dairy note. Isolate tends to taste cleaner and thinner than concentrate, because most of the fat and lactose has been filtered out. Concentrate, with a little milk fat left in, tastes slightly richer and creamier. Mixed with water alone, most buyers find either one bland but drinkable; mixed with milk, coffee or fruit, most people say they barely notice it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unflavored plant protein is a different story. Pea protein, the most common plant base, is widely described in buyer reviews as earthy, beany and slightly bitter, with a heavier, more gritty texture than whey. Brown rice protein is chalkier; hemp is grassy and green. Almost nobody drinks unflavored pea protein with water by choice. In smoothies with banana and berries, or in chili, soups and savory baking, it works well, and many plant users say they prefer controlling the flavor themselves.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
